 xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"57.44606018066406\" y=\"451.3406982421875\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#666666\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(79.44606018066406 463.3406982421875)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> provision of medicinal plants</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"57.44606018066406\" y=\"483.3406982421875\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(79.44606018066406 495.3406982421875)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> improvement of neighborhood</text>,<text transform=\"translate(79.44606018066406 519.3406982421875)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> attachment</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"57.44606018066406\" y=\"539.3406982421875\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(79.44606018066406 551.3406982421875)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> improvement of local microclimate</text></g></svg></figure><div class=\"sr-only\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for bar graph titled Average Ratings of Urban Agriculture’s Contribution to Various Social or Ecological Services\"><ul>\n<li>For each data category, the following bars are shown:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>provision of medicinal plants</li>\n<li>improvement of neighborhood attachment</li>\n<li>improvement of local microclimate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The data for the 3 categories are as follows:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>project leaders:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>provision of medicinal plants: 2.0</li>\n<li>improvement of neighborhood attachment: 3.0</li>\n<li>improvement of local microclimate: 3.0</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>stakeholders:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>provision of medicinal plants: 3.6</li>\n<li>improvement of neighborhood attachment: 4.1</li>\n<li>improvement of local microclimate: 4.1</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>general public:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>provision of medicinal plants: 4.6</li>\n<li>improvement of neighborhood attachment: 4.3</li>\n<li>improvement of local microclimate: 3.8</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>Urban agriculture is the practice of growing plant- or animal-based products in urban settings such as community gardens and rooftop farms. Esther Sany&eacute;-Mengual, Kathrin Specht, and their team surveyed three groups of people in Bologna, Italy&mdash;leaders of urban agriculture projects, stakeholders in urban agriculture (<span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"e g\">e.g.</span>, food researchers and urban farming associations), and the general public&mdash;to compare their views about the extent to which urban agriculture contributes to 25 social or ecological services that the team identified. The survey results show that, on average, project leaders rated urban agriculture as contributing less to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the assertion?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4a6dc013-222f-40d8-a4ce-0d5fcc1743b5","content":"<p>providing medicinal plants than to improving the local microclimate.</p>"},{"id":"23551d07-3017-4f2e-a8e8-0540d33944d6","content":"<p>improving community members&rsquo; sense of attachment to the neighborhood than to offering educational opportunities.</p>"},{"id":"d97de3b3-270c-4cef-917a-a17a6db9f372","content":"<p>improving the local microclimate than to providing medicinal plants.</p>"},{"id":"46e059a9-4836-47ce-a307-a6aacb1f22bf","content":"<p>improving the local microclimate than to providing tourist attractions.</p>"}],"keys":["4a6dc013-222f-40d8-a4ce-0d5fcc1743b5"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the assertion about urban agriculture. The graph shows the average survey ratings, on a scale of 0 to 5, from three groups of people&mdash;project leaders, stakeholders, and the general public&mdash;concerning the contributions of urban agriculture. According to the graph, project leaders gave an average rating of approximately 2 to urban agriculture&rsquo;s contributions to the provision of medicinal plants, which is lower than the average rating of approximately 3 that project leaders gave to urban agriculture&rsquo;s contributions to both improving the local microclimate and improving a sense of attachment to the neighborhood. This shows that project leaders rated urban agriculture as contributing less to providing medicinal plants than to improving the local microclimate.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the graph doesn&rsquo;t provide data about urban agriculture&rsquo;s contribution to educational opportunities; therefore, this comparison can&rsquo;t be made. Choice C is incorrect because it contradicts the data shown in the graph. On average, project leaders gave a rating of approximately 3 to urban agriculture&rsquo;s contributions to improving the local microclimate, which is higher, not lower, than the average rating of approximately 2 that they gave to its contributions to providing medicinal plants. Choice D is incorrect because the graph doesn&rsquo;t provide data about urban agriculture&rsquo;s contribution to providing tourist attractions; therefore, this comparison can&rsquo;t be made.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061189,"updateDate":1755117061189}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061189, 1755117061189),
    ($SATQ$01de46bb$SATQ$, $SATQ$1b1c4ea0-13e3-4b12-b816-9f277ccd9641$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Menominee and Anishinaabe peoples have been growing wild rice&mdash;known as <span lang="oj"><em>manoomin</em></span> in the Ojibwe language&mdash;in the Great Lakes region of North America for centuries, but climatic changes are causing lakes to get deeper, thereby threatening wild rice. These plants are extremely <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> to water depth during the &ldquo;floating leaf&rdquo; stage of development, and if the water is too deep, the buoyancy of the young wild-rice plants can literally uproot them from the lake bottom, destroying them.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b7c794ce-4aa3-41d0-aa5c-fff38406feb4","content":"<p>immune</p>"},{"id":"f2521ed3-2a39-44b9-9194-88f51af22fc8","content":"<p>sensitive</p>"},{"id":"9a612058-2ce5-422f-a6d9-2770f3aca2db","content":"<p>limited</p>"},{"id":"538802a9-80d7-48d9-8de2-c0fea059adb9","content":"<p>receptive</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f2521ed3-2a39-44b9-9194-88f51af22fc8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of wild rice. As used in this context, "sensitive" means vulnerable or easily affected. The text indicates that climate change is causing the lakes to get deeper, which consequently threatens the wild rice that grows in the area. The text goes on to state that deep water can cause young wild-rice plants to uproot from the bottom of the lake. This context supports the idea that the plants are sensitive to water depth during early stages of development.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "immune" means protected or shielded, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. The text explains that deep water can cause young wild-rice plants to become uprooted and destroyed, indicating that these plants are harmed by deep water, not protected from it. Choice C is incorrect because "limited" means confined or restrained, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. The text explains that water depth can negatively affect young wild-rice plants but doesn&rsquo;t suggest that water depth limits or restrains the plants. Choice D is incorrect because "receptive" means open or tolerant, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. The text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the wild-rice plants are open to or tolerant of an increase in water depth but rather the opposite: an increase in water depth can harm young wild-rice plants.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"01de46bb","external_id":"1b1c4ea0-13e3-4b12-b816-9f277ccd9641","vaultid":null,"uId":"b3ca441a-ecca-4c73-a351-6e067daf67c3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Menominee and Anishinaabe peoples have been growing wild rice&mdash;known as <span lang=\"oj\"><em>manoomin</em></span> in the Ojibwe language&mdash;in the Great Lakes region of North America for centuries, but climatic changes are causing lakes to get deeper, thereby threatening wild rice. These plants are extremely <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> to water depth during the &ldquo;floating leaf&rdquo; stage of development, and if the water is too deep, the buoyancy of the young wild-rice plants can literally uproot them from the lake bottom, destroying them.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b7c794ce-4aa3-41d0-aa5c-fff38406feb4","content":"<p>immune</p>"},{"id":"f2521ed3-2a39-44b9-9194-88f51af22fc8","content":"<p>sensitive</p>"},{"id":"9a612058-2ce5-422f-a6d9-2770f3aca2db","content":"<p>limited</p>"},{"id":"538802a9-80d7-48d9-8de2-c0fea059adb9","content":"<p>receptive</p>"}],"keys":["f2521ed3-2a39-44b9-9194-88f51af22fc8"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of wild rice. As used in this context, \"sensitive\" means vulnerable or easily affected. The text indicates that climate change is causing the lakes to get deeper, which consequently threatens the wild rice that grows in the area. The text goes on to state that deep water can cause young wild-rice plants to uproot from the bottom of the lake. This context supports the idea that the plants are sensitive to water depth during early stages of development.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"immune\" means protected or shielded, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. The text explains that deep water can cause young wild-rice plants to become uprooted and destroyed, indicating that these plants are harmed by deep water, not protected from it. Choice C is incorrect because \"limited\" means confined or restrained, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. The text explains that water depth can negatively affect young wild-rice plants but doesn&rsquo;t suggest that water depth limits or restrains the plants. Choice D is incorrect because \"receptive\" means open or tolerant, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. The text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the wild-rice plants are open to or tolerant of an increase in water depth but rather the opposite: an increase in water depth can harm young wild-rice plants.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323036,"updateDate":1730147323036}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323036, 1730147323036),
    ($SATQ$02e49a0c$SATQ$, $SATQ$7574368f-8d8b-4b9e-a7f9-24990a5a1e0d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$435e49f6-86fb-4e0f-8bcc-25589e227ed6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Genetic studies have led researchers to suggest that turtles are most closely related to the group that includes modern crocodiles. But studies of fossils have suggested instead that turtles are most closely related to other groups, such as the one that contains modern snakes. <u>However, many of the fossil studies have relied on incomplete data sets.</u>&nbsp;For a 2022 investigation, biologist Tiago R. Sim&otilde;es and colleagues examined more than 1,000 reptile fossils collected worldwide. From this large data set, they found clear agreement with the results of the genetic studies.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a473b2ea-f6a5-4210-95bd-0b60af499f93","content":"<p>It offers an overview of the tools scientists use to examine fossils.</p>"},{"id":"0490f396-9aaa-4691-bf13-c737663b83a9","content":"<p>It describes a limitation of some studies about the origin of turtles.</p>"},{"id":"c3cb5286-916a-48f0-b5b7-23be04933d40","content":"<p>It summarizes previous research on the evolution of crocodiles.</p>"},{"id":"841cfa54-2c83-4774-b595-5337f411ae21","content":"<p>It criticizes a widely held belief about genetic studies of reptiles.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0490f396-9aaa-4691-bf13-c737663b83a9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence mentions that some fossil studies have relied on incomplete data sets, suggesting that these studies are limited in what they can tell us about turtles&rsquo; origins.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence mentions the incompleteness of the data sets studied, it doesn&rsquo;t mention any tools or techniques used to examine fossils. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence doesn&rsquo;t mention anything about the evolution of crocodiles. Choice D is incorrect. This sentence doesn&rsquo;t directly mention or criticize any "widely held belief," and it focuses on a limitation of fossil studies of reptiles, not genetic studies.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-E010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$f129b4f6-11b1-42b8-92f3-a38eac1bc47f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"02e49a0c","external_id":"7574368f-8d8b-4b9e-a7f9-24990a5a1e0d","vaultid":"435e49f6-86fb-4e0f-8bcc-25589e227ed6","uId":"d0188b1a-6020-4e1a-b729-9c623cb8ae21","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Genetic studies have led researchers to suggest that turtles are most closely related to the group that includes modern crocodiles. But studies of fossils have suggested instead that turtles are most closely related to other groups, such as the one that contains modern snakes. <u>However, many of the fossil studies have relied on incomplete data sets.</u>&nbsp;For a 2022 investigation, biologist Tiago R. Sim&otilde;es and colleagues examined more than 1,000 reptile fossils collected worldwide. From this large data set, they found clear agreement with the results of the genetic studies.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a473b2ea-f6a5-4210-95bd-0b60af499f93","content":"<p>It offers an overview of the tools scientists use to examine fossils.</p>"},{"id":"0490f396-9aaa-4691-bf13-c737663b83a9","content":"<p>It describes a limitation of some studies about the origin of turtles.</p>"},{"id":"c3cb5286-916a-48f0-b5b7-23be04933d40","content":"<p>It summarizes previous research on the evolution of crocodiles.</p>"},{"id":"841cfa54-2c83-4774-b595-5337f411ae21","content":"<p>It criticizes a widely held belief about genetic studies of reptiles.</p>"}],"keys":["0490f396-9aaa-4691-bf13-c737663b83a9"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence mentions that some fossil studies have relied on incomplete data sets, suggesting that these studies are limited in what they can tell us about turtles&rsquo; origins.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence mentions the incompleteness of the data sets studied, it doesn&rsquo;t mention any tools or techniques used to examine fossils. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence doesn&rsquo;t mention anything about the evolution of crocodiles. Choice D is incorrect. This sentence doesn&rsquo;t directly mention or criticize any \"widely held belief,\" and it focuses on a limitation of fossil studies of reptiles, not genetic studies.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-E010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"f129b4f6-11b1-42b8-92f3-a38eac1bc47f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959375,"updateDate":1691007959375}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959375, 1691007959375),
    ($SATQ$02fd3da7$SATQ$, $SATQ$f952519c-47b4-4050-b3b8-3934f05c1835$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$35922aea-c514-40f0-a7dc-0910aa8df5a5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><span role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Public policy researcher Anthony Fowler studied the history of elections in Australia, a country that requires citizens to vote. Fowler argues that requiring citizens to vote leads to a significant increase in voters who would otherwise not have the time or motivation to vote. Thus, election results in countries that require citizens to vote better reflect the preferences of the country as a whole.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>Governments in democratic countries function better when more people vote. However, forcing people to vote may have negative consequences. Shane P. Singh and Jason Roy studied what happens when a country requires its citizens to vote. They found that when people feel forced to vote, they tend to spend less time looking for information about their choices when voting. As a result, votes from these voters may not reflect their actual preferences.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would Singh and Roy (Text 2) most likely respond to the research discussed in Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"75f17114-1947-46c0-af46-b3a14063eca5","content":"<p>Only countries of a certain population size should implement mandatory voting.</p>"},{"id":"8cf40bb7-f7d8-4c01-ade2-8fbc004bfe5e","content":"<p>People who are forced to vote are likely to become politically engaged in other ways, such as volunteering or running for office.</p>"},{"id":"7af6b177-53ad-4d9f-972c-9a5c178539a9","content":"<p>Requiring people to vote does not necessarily lead to election outcomes that better represent the preferences of the country as a whole.</p>"},{"id":"6488417e-d87d-476a-86e4-62e30b74b5c1","content":"<p>Countries that require voting must also make the process of voting easier for their citizens.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7af6b177-53ad-4d9f-972c-9a5c178539a9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. Text 1 claims that mandatory voting results in elections that "better reflect the preferences of the country." Singh and Roy disagree. They claim that more voters doesn&rsquo;t equal more quality votes&mdash;instead, they argue that forced voting may lead to less-informed votes that "may not reflect [voters&rsquo;] actual preferences."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Neither text mentions the population size of countries that require voting, or how that might affect election outcomes. Choice B is incorrect. Neither text discusses the effects of mandatory voting on other forms of political engagement. Choice D is incorrect. Neither text discusses the ease or difficulty of the voting process in countries that require voting.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-E007$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b8e1421f-decd-4d64-aca2-301e5ef9902f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"02fd3da7","external_id":"f952519c-47b4-4050-b3b8-3934f05c1835","vaultid":"35922aea-c514-40f0-a7dc-0910aa8df5a5","uId":"0db9d53f-cee0-463f-8433-36a1731ce5cc","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Public policy researcher Anthony Fowler studied the history of elections in Australia, a country that requires citizens to vote. Fowler argues that requiring citizens to vote leads to a significant increase in voters who would otherwise not have the time or motivation to vote. Thus, election results in countries that require citizens to vote better reflect the preferences of the country as a whole.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>Governments in democratic countries function better when more people vote. However, forcing people to vote may have negative consequences. Shane P. Singh and Jason Roy studied what happens when a country requires its citizens to vote. They found that when people feel forced to vote, they tend to spend less time looking for information about their choices when voting. As a result, votes from these voters may not reflect their actual preferences.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would Singh and Roy (Text 2) most likely respond to the research discussed in Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"75f17114-1947-46c0-af46-b3a14063eca5","content":"<p>Only countries of a certain population size should implement mandatory voting.</p>"},{"id":"8cf40bb7-f7d8-4c01-ade2-8fbc004bfe5e","content":"<p>People who are forced to vote are likely to become politically engaged in other ways, such as volunteering or running for office.</p>"},{"id":"7af6b177-53ad-4d9f-972c-9a5c178539a9","content":"<p>Requiring people to vote does not necessarily lead to election outcomes that better represent the preferences of the country as a whole.</p>"},{"id":"6488417e-d87d-476a-86e4-62e30b74b5c1","content":"<p>Countries that require voting must also make the process of voting easier for their citizens.</p>"}],"keys":["7af6b177-53ad-4d9f-972c-9a5c178539a9"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. Text 1 claims that mandatory voting results in elections that \"better reflect the preferences of the country.\" Singh and Roy disagree. They claim that more voters doesn&rsquo;t equal more quality votes&mdash;instead, they argue that forced voting may lead to less-informed votes that \"may not reflect [voters&rsquo;] actual preferences.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Neither text mentions the population size of countries that require voting, or how that might affect election outcomes. Choice B is incorrect. Neither text discusses the effects of mandatory voting on other forms of political engagement. Choice D is incorrect. Neither text discusses the ease or difficulty of the voting process in countries that require voting.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-E007","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b8e1421f-decd-4d64-aca2-301e5ef9902f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959361,"updateDate":1691007959361}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959361, 1691007959361),
    ($SATQ$03080769$SATQ$, $SATQ$c232aa2c-2e8a-487c-9daa-505a57ba1aab$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f9ad26c4-6d32-4052-a8be-afa81411f387$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><strong><span role="heading" aria-level="2">Text 1</span></strong></p>
<p>Philosopher G.E. Moore&rsquo;s most influential work entails the concept of common sense. He asserts that there are certain beliefs that all people, including philosophers, know instinctively to be true, whether or not they profess otherwise: among them, that they have bodies, or that they exist in a world with other objects that have three dimensions. Moore&rsquo;s careful work on common sense may seem obvious but was in fact groundbreaking.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong><span role="heading" aria-level="2">Text 2</span></strong></p>
<p>External world skepticism is a philosophical stance supposing that we cannot be sure of the existence of anything outside our own minds. During a lecture, G.E. Moore once offered a proof refuting this stance by holding out his hands and saying, &ldquo;Here is one hand, and here is another.&rdquo; Many philosophers reflexively reject this proof (Annalisa Coliva called it &ldquo;an obviously annoying failure&rdquo;) but have found it a challenge to articulate exactly why the proof fails.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 1 most likely respond to proponents of the philosophical stance outlined in Text 2?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2ea4b998-90ce-4ceb-be59-9166d28a7ece","content":"<p>By agreeing with those proponents that Moore&rsquo;s treatment of positions that contradict his own is fundamentally unserious</p>"},{"id":"599bcd5b-6824-4400-a274-c949734d947c","content":"<p>By suggesting that an instinctive distaste for Moore&rsquo;s position is preventing external world skeptics from constructing a sufficiently rigorous refutation of Moore</p>"},{"id":"4e04833a-e3cc-4c39-96ed-72c2921c02c4","content":"<p>By arguing that if it is valid to assert that some facts are true based on instinct, it is also valid to assert that some proofs are inadequate based on instinct</p>"},{"id":"157f737c-18f9-4708-84ed-f020dea22959","content":"<p>By pointing out that Moore would assert that external world skepticism is at odds with other beliefs those proponents must unavoidably hold</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["157f737c-18f9-4708-84ed-f020dea22959"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. According to the author of Text 1, Moore&rsquo;s definition of common sense&mdash;things we instinctively know are true&mdash;includes the belief that we all &ldquo;exist in a world with other objects.&rdquo; The author of Text 1 describes this notion as both &ldquo;obvious&rdquo; and &ldquo;groundbreaking.&rdquo; &nbsp;So it&rsquo;s safe to infer that the author would observe that Moore would respond to external world skeptics by arguing that since everyone instinctively knows that things exist outside of their own minds, then external world skepticism must be wrong. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that the author of Text 1 would respond this way to external world skeptics. If anything, the author of Text 1 seems to agree with Moore. Choice B is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that the author of Text 1 would respond this way to external world skeptics. The author of Text 1 never mentions external world skeptics directly, let alone why they have a hard time refuting Moore&rsquo;s position. Choice C is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that the author of Text 1 would respond this way to external world skeptics. Text 1&rsquo;s presentation of Moore&rsquo;s concept of common sense only includes the idea that some facts are true based on instinct&mdash;it doesn&rsquo;t mention the idea that some proofs are inadequate based on instinct. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-H002$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$6e6128b9-e855-498a-9e58-91fa6fbf9a71$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"03080769","external_id":"c232aa2c-2e8a-487c-9daa-505a57ba1aab","vaultid":"f9ad26c4-6d32-4052-a8be-afa81411f387","uId":"9b42bfd5-b42d-408e-9328-d7f4540e32ab","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><strong><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\">Text 1</span></strong></p>\n<p>Philosopher G.E. Moore&rsquo;s most influential work entails the concept of common sense. He asserts that there are certain beliefs that all people, including philosophers, know instinctively to be true, whether or not they profess otherwise: among them, that they have bodies, or that they exist in a world with other objects that have three dimensions. Moore&rsquo;s careful work on common sense may seem obvious but was in fact groundbreaking.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\">Text 2</span></strong></p>\n<p>External world skepticism is a philosophical stance supposing that we cannot be sure of the existence of anything outside our own minds. During a lecture, G.E. Moore once offered a proof refuting this stance by holding out his hands and saying, &ldquo;Here is one hand, and here is another.&rdquo; Many philosophers reflexively reject this proof (Annalisa Coliva called it &ldquo;an obviously annoying failure&rdquo;) but have found it a challenge to articulate exactly why the proof fails.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 1 most likely respond to proponents of the philosophical stance outlined in Text 2?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2ea4b998-90ce-4ceb-be59-9166d28a7ece","content":"<p>By agreeing with those proponents that Moore&rsquo;s treatment of positions that contradict his own is fundamentally unserious</p>"},{"id":"599bcd5b-6824-4400-a274-c949734d947c","content":"<p>By suggesting that an instinctive distaste for Moore&rsquo;s position is preventing external world skeptics from constructing a sufficiently rigorous refutation of Moore</p>"},{"id":"4e04833a-e3cc-4c39-96ed-72c2921c02c4","content":"<p>By arguing that if it is valid to assert that some facts are true based on instinct, it is also valid to assert that some proofs are inadequate based on instinct</p>"},{"id":"157f737c-18f9-4708-84ed-f020dea22959","content":"<p>By pointing out that Moore would assert that external world skepticism is at odds with other beliefs those proponents must unavoidably hold</p>"}],"keys":["157f737c-18f9-4708-84ed-f020dea22959"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. According to the author of Text 1, Moore&rsquo;s definition of common sense&mdash;things we instinctively know are true&mdash;includes the belief that we all &ldquo;exist in a world with other objects.&rdquo; The author of Text 1 describes this notion as both &ldquo;obvious&rdquo; and &ldquo;groundbreaking.&rdquo; &nbsp;So it&rsquo;s safe to infer that the author would observe that Moore would respond to external world skeptics by arguing that since everyone instinctively knows that things exist outside of their own minds, then external world skepticism must be wrong. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that the author of Text 1 would respond this way to external world skeptics. If anything, the author of Text 1 seems to agree with Moore. Choice B is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that the author of Text 1 would respond this way to external world skeptics. The author of Text 1 never mentions external world skeptics directly, let alone why they have a hard time refuting Moore&rsquo;s position. Choice C is incorrect. We can&rsquo;t infer that the author of Text 1 would respond this way to external world skeptics. Text 1&rsquo;s presentation of Moore&rsquo;s concept of common sense only includes the idea that some facts are true based on instinct&mdash;it doesn&rsquo;t mention the idea that some proofs are inadequate based on instinct. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-H002","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"6e6128b9-e855-498a-9e58-91fa6fbf9a71","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959365,"updateDate":1691007959365}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959365, 1691007959365),
    ($SATQ$03c9f327$SATQ$, $SATQ$54287047-9ca3-4931-b6e4-13c25ad6e14c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$239baf5a-6a07-4596-afa1-5bcd55c0b872$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Charlotte Bront&euml;&rsquo;s 1847 novel <em>Jane Eyre</em>. Jane, the narrator, works as a governess at Thornfield Hall.</p>
<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>I went on with my day&rsquo;s business tranquilly; but ever and anon vague suggestions kept wandering across my brain of reasons why I should quit Thornfield; and I kept involuntarily framing advertisements and pondering conjectures about new situations: these thoughts I did not think to check; they might germinate and bear fruit if they could.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5f7a8282-2703-4a8f-984e-d445ed413fbd","content":"<p>To convey a contrast between Jane&rsquo;s outward calmness and internal restlessness</p>"},{"id":"bed3c021-4c9c-4855-aeb1-e00598467f91","content":"<p>To emphasize Jane&rsquo;s loyalty to the people she works for at Thornfield Hall</p>"},{"id":"be69795a-2450-4c15-9d83-cf007d589000","content":"<p>To demonstrate that Jane finds her situation both challenging and deeply fulfilling</p>"},{"id":"2b08badc-2b96-4b15-89e2-389c3602e5a7","content":"<p>To describe Jane&rsquo;s determination to secure employment outside of Thornfield Hall</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5f7a8282-2703-4a8f-984e-d445ed413fbd"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text, which is to show that while Jane calmly goes about her daily tasks, she is experiencing internal agitation about possibly seeking a new job. At the start of the text, Jane says, &ldquo;I went on with my day&rsquo;s business tranquilly,&rdquo; indicating that she is outwardly calm. This outward calmness is then contrasted with her intense internal restlessness, as Jane says that thoughts of leaving her job keep running through her mind, that she is &ldquo;involuntarily framing advertisements&rdquo; (meaning that she can&rsquo;t stop herself from thinking up potential listings for jobs), and that she often wonders what new &ldquo;situations&rdquo; (or jobs) would be like.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text gives no indication of Jane&rsquo;s feelings, either positive or negative, about the people she works for at Thornfield Hall. And rather than emphasizing that Jane feels particularly loyal to her employers, the text focuses on her constant consideration of leaving her job. Choice C is incorrect because the text gives no indication that Jane finds her current situation fulfilling, or satisfying. Given that much of the text is focused on Jane&rsquo;s thoughts about possibly leaving her job for a new one, it might be the case that she finds her situation challenging, but there is no evidence in the text that Jane also finds that situation satisfying&mdash;she says nothing positive about her current job at all, in fact. Choice D is incorrect because the text describes Jane as wondering about getting a new job, not as determined to definitely do so. Jane keeps thinking about reasons why she &ldquo;should&rdquo; quit her current job (indicating that she hasn&rsquo;t yet decided to) and imagining possible new situations she could find, but she says at the end of the text that these thoughts &ldquo;might germinate and bear fruit if they could,&rdquo; meaning that the thoughts haven&rsquo;t yet led to a decision&mdash;that Jane isn&rsquo;t yet determined to get a new job somewhere else. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-021-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e1880ce2-9ce8-4dfa-82b7-1ce117b24d68$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"03c9f327","external_id":"54287047-9ca3-4931-b6e4-13c25ad6e14c","vaultid":"239baf5a-6a07-4596-afa1-5bcd55c0b872","uId":"1eec9ae0-8d1c-4de1-8b23-b633d3775c78","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Charlotte Bront&euml;&rsquo;s 1847 novel <em>Jane Eyre</em>. Jane, the narrator, works as a governess at Thornfield Hall.</p>\n<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>I went on with my day&rsquo;s business tranquilly; but ever and anon vague suggestions kept wandering across my brain of reasons why I should quit Thornfield; and I kept involuntarily framing advertisements and pondering conjectures about new situations: these thoughts I did not think to check; they might germinate and bear fruit if they could.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5f7a8282-2703-4a8f-984e-d445ed413fbd","content":"<p>To convey a contrast between Jane&rsquo;s outward calmness and internal restlessness</p>"},{"id":"bed3c021-4c9c-4855-aeb1-e00598467f91","content":"<p>To emphasize Jane&rsquo;s loyalty to the people she works for at Thornfield Hall</p>"},{"id":"be69795a-2450-4c15-9d83-cf007d589000","content":"<p>To demonstrate that Jane finds her situation both challenging and deeply fulfilling</p>"},{"id":"2b08badc-2b96-4b15-89e2-389c3602e5a7","content":"<p>To describe Jane&rsquo;s determination to secure employment outside of Thornfield Hall</p>"}],"keys":["5f7a8282-2703-4a8f-984e-d445ed413fbd"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text, which is to show that while Jane calmly goes about her daily tasks, she is experiencing internal agitation about possibly seeking a new job. At the start of the text, Jane says, &ldquo;I went on with my day&rsquo;s business tranquilly,&rdquo; indicating that she is outwardly calm. This outward calmness is then contrasted with her intense internal restlessness, as Jane says that thoughts of leaving her job keep running through her mind, that she is &ldquo;involuntarily framing advertisements&rdquo; (meaning that she can&rsquo;t stop herself from thinking up potential listings for jobs), and that she often wonders what new &ldquo;situations&rdquo; (or jobs) would be like.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text gives no indication of Jane&rsquo;s feelings, either positive or negative, about the people she works for at Thornfield Hall. And rather than emphasizing that Jane feels particularly loyal to her employers, the text focuses on her constant consideration of leaving her job. Choice C is incorrect because the text gives no indication that Jane finds her current situation fulfilling, or satisfying. Given that much of the text is focused on Jane&rsquo;s thoughts about possibly leaving her job for a new one, it might be the case that she finds her situation challenging, but there is no evidence in the text that Jane also finds that situation satisfying&mdash;she says nothing positive about her current job at all, in fact. Choice D is incorrect because the text describes Jane as wondering about getting a new job, not as determined to definitely do so. Jane keeps thinking about reasons why she &ldquo;should&rdquo; quit her current job (indicating that she hasn&rsquo;t yet decided to) and imagining possible new situations she could find, but she says at the end of the text that these thoughts &ldquo;might germinate and bear fruit if they could,&rdquo; meaning that the thoughts haven&rsquo;t yet led to a decision&mdash;that Jane isn&rsquo;t yet determined to get a new job somewhere else. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-021-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e1880ce2-9ce8-4dfa-82b7-1ce117b24d68","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959317,"updateDate":1691007959317}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959317, 1691007959317),
    ($SATQ$03eeecf3$SATQ$, $SATQ$5a42da90-a3d0-434f-a53f-0f77b8faa19e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from <span data-ssml-sub-alias="H D&rsquo;s">H.D.&rsquo;s</span> 1916 poem &ldquo;Mid-Day.&rdquo; In the poem, the speaker is on a path in an outdoor setting.</p>
<blockquote class="dap_poemexcerpt">
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">A slight wind shakes the seed-pods</span>&mdash;</p>
<p>my thoughts are spent</p>
<p>as the black seeds.</p>
<p>My thoughts tear me,</p>
<p>I dread their fever.</p>
<p>I am scattered in its whirl.</p>
<p>I am scattered like</p>
<p>the hot shrivelled seeds.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a43da980-3141-482e-b52a-bedbd63abf55","content":"<p>It illustrates a change in the natural environment that the speaker implies is responsible for the growing misgivings described in the text.</p>"},{"id":"b43b488d-3f58-4fb4-9704-45bce55e0cfb","content":"<p>It establishes an example of consistency in the natural landscape that the speaker then contrasts with the unpredictability of human emotions.</p>"},{"id":"d0467004-155a-41c6-a69b-201f54805c48","content":"<p>It presents an observation of an occurrence in the natural world that the speaker then expands on to convey a sense of a turbulent interior state.</p>"},{"id":"fb0bb58c-cea0-4d2b-ba6b-e95ad23aa73a","content":"<p>It evokes the ordinariness of an event in nature to suggest that the critical self-evaluation the speaker engages in is a common pursuit.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d0467004-155a-41c6-a69b-201f54805c48"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole. In the text, the speaker mentions the occurrence in nature of seedpods being shaken by a slight wind. The speaker then goes on to compare the black seeds to thoughts, using language that indicates that the speaker&rsquo;s state of mind is unsettled (e.g., "my thoughts are spent"; "My thoughts tear me, I dread their fever"). The text concludes with a comparison between the speaker&rsquo;s "scattered" state of mind and the "hot shrivelled seeds." Thus, the underlined portion of the text presents an observation of an occurrence in the natural world that the speaker then expands on to convey a sense of a turbulent interior state.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text does not indicate that the seedpods are the cause of the speaker&rsquo;s state of mind; thus, they could not be responsible for any misgivings the speaker has. Choice B is incorrect because the text does not contrast the natural landscape with the speaker&rsquo;s state of mind or describe the wind shaking the seedpods as consistent; rather, the text suggests that the state of the natural world and the speaker&rsquo;s state of mind are similar in that both are unsettled. Choice D is incorrect because there is no indication in the text that the speaker regularly engages in critical self-evaluation, only that in this particular instance the speaker&rsquo;s state of mind is turbulent.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"03eeecf3","external_id":"5a42da90-a3d0-434f-a53f-0f77b8faa19e","vaultid":null,"uId":"f5c6397f-331d-43b2-a866-f5e107c938a2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"H D&rsquo;s\">H.D.&rsquo;s</span> 1916 poem &ldquo;Mid-Day.&rdquo; In the poem, the speaker is on a path in an outdoor setting.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"dap_poemexcerpt\">\n<p><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">A slight wind shakes the seed-pods</span>&mdash;</p>\n<p>my thoughts are spent</p>\n<p>as the black seeds.</p>\n<p>My thoughts tear me,</p>\n<p>I dread their fever.</p>\n<p>I am scattered in its whirl.</p>\n<p>I am scattered like</p>\n<p>the hot shrivelled seeds.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a43da980-3141-482e-b52a-bedbd63abf55","content":"<p>It illustrates a change in the natural environment that the speaker implies is responsible for the growing misgivings described in the text.</p>"},{"id":"b43b488d-3f58-4fb4-9704-45bce55e0cfb","content":"<p>It establishes an example of consistency in the natural landscape that the speaker then contrasts with the unpredictability of human emotions.</p>"},{"id":"d0467004-155a-41c6-a69b-201f54805c48","content":"<p>It presents an observation of an occurrence in the natural world that the speaker then expands on to convey a sense of a turbulent interior state.</p>"},{"id":"fb0bb58c-cea0-4d2b-ba6b-e95ad23aa73a","content":"<p>It evokes the ordinariness of an event in nature to suggest that the critical self-evaluation the speaker engages in is a common pursuit.</p>"}],"keys":["d0467004-155a-41c6-a69b-201f54805c48"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole. In the text, the speaker mentions the occurrence in nature of seedpods being shaken by a slight wind. The speaker then goes on to compare the black seeds to thoughts, using language that indicates that the speaker&rsquo;s state of mind is unsettled (e.g., \"my thoughts are spent\"; \"My thoughts tear me, I dread their fever\"). The text concludes with a comparison between the speaker&rsquo;s \"scattered\" state of mind and the \"hot shrivelled seeds.\" Thus, the underlined portion of the text presents an observation of an occurrence in the natural world that the speaker then expands on to convey a sense of a turbulent interior state.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text does not indicate that the seedpods are the cause of the speaker&rsquo;s state of mind; thus, they could not be responsible for any misgivings the speaker has. Choice B is incorrect because the text does not contrast the natural landscape with the speaker&rsquo;s state of mind or describe the wind shaking the seedpods as consistent; rather, the text suggests that the state of the natural world and the speaker&rsquo;s state of mind are similar in that both are unsettled. Choice D is incorrect because there is no indication in the text that the speaker regularly engages in critical self-evaluation, only that in this particular instance the speaker&rsquo;s state of mind is turbulent.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669207,"updateDate":1743430669207}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669207, 1743430669207),
    ($SATQ$03f5a9b7$SATQ$, $SATQ$e5f96c7a-1d41-4770-b369-48b1a28e1456$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>When marine biologist Brittany Williams played different ocean recordings to groups of oyster larvae, groups that heard sounds of a healthy oyster reef were the most <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>. They were twice as likely as other groups to show signs of making a permanent home. This suggests that playing recordings of a healthy ocean reef may encourage oysters to create such a reef.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"caa61e3e-738a-4e1a-bfdb-3770b3cd5e1c","content":"<p>responsive</p>"},{"id":"e732a7b1-3869-48c0-bc82-f880212d5357","content":"<p>inactive</p>"},{"id":"5c3a8884-6726-4d02-8047-962a93f6b690","content":"<p>liked</p>"},{"id":"de3b7200-f3d2-4761-8fd7-e8eb3380e8ad","content":"<p>distressed</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["caa61e3e-738a-4e1a-bfdb-3770b3cd5e1c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of oysters and ocean recordings. As used in this context, "responsive" means reacting positively. The text presents a finding and describes it as suggesting that healthy ocean sounds may encourage oyster larvae to establish a reef (a permanent home): groups of oyster larvae that heard recorded sounds of a healthy oyster reef were two times more likely to show signs of forming their own reef than groups that heard different ocean recordings were. This context conveys that the groups that heard healthy reef sounds had a more positive reaction than the other groups did&mdash;that is, they were the most responsive.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that hearing sounds of a healthy oyster reef caused some groups of oyster larvae to become "inactive," or to stop engaging in work or activity. Instead, the text conveys that the groups that heard those sounds were observed engaging in activity: they were two times more likely to create a reef (a permanent home) than groups that heard different ocean sounds were. Choice C is incorrect because describing the groups of oyster larvae that heard sounds of a healthy oyster reef as most "liked," or regarded with pleasure, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text focuses on the idea that a study found that healthy reef sounds may encourage groups of oyster larvae to form reefs (permanent homes), not on the idea that some of the groups in the study were liked more than others. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that hearing sounds of a healthy oyster reef caused some groups of oyster larvae to be "distressed," or to experience difficulty or begin to decline. Instead, the text indicates that the sounds increased the likelihood that groups of larvae would establish a reef (a permanent home), suggesting that the sounds had a positive effect on the larvae.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"03f5a9b7","external_id":"e5f96c7a-1d41-4770-b369-48b1a28e1456","vaultid":null,"uId":"6aaa4176-7920-473c-946e-e1959f3332d4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>When marine biologist Brittany Williams played different ocean recordings to groups of oyster larvae, groups that heard sounds of a healthy oyster reef were the most <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>. They were twice as likely as other groups to show signs of making a permanent home. This suggests that playing recordings of a healthy ocean reef may encourage oysters to create such a reef.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"caa61e3e-738a-4e1a-bfdb-3770b3cd5e1c","content":"<p>responsive</p>"},{"id":"e732a7b1-3869-48c0-bc82-f880212d5357","content":"<p>inactive</p>"},{"id":"5c3a8884-6726-4d02-8047-962a93f6b690","content":"<p>liked</p>"},{"id":"de3b7200-f3d2-4761-8fd7-e8eb3380e8ad","content":"<p>distressed</p>"}],"keys":["caa61e3e-738a-4e1a-bfdb-3770b3cd5e1c"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of oysters and ocean recordings. As used in this context, \"responsive\" means reacting positively. The text presents a finding and describes it as suggesting that healthy ocean sounds may encourage oyster larvae to establish a reef (a permanent home): groups of oyster larvae that heard recorded sounds of a healthy oyster reef were two times more likely to show signs of forming their own reef than groups that heard different ocean recordings were. This context conveys that the groups that heard healthy reef sounds had a more positive reaction than the other groups did&mdash;that is, they were the most responsive.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that hearing sounds of a healthy oyster reef caused some groups of oyster larvae to become \"inactive,\" or to stop engaging in work or activity. Instead, the text conveys that the groups that heard those sounds were observed engaging in activity: they were two times more likely to create a reef (a permanent home) than groups that heard different ocean sounds were. Choice C is incorrect because describing the groups of oyster larvae that heard sounds of a healthy oyster reef as most \"liked,\" or regarded with pleasure, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text focuses on the idea that a study found that healthy reef sounds may encourage groups of oyster larvae to form reefs (permanent homes), not on the idea that some of the groups in the study were liked more than others. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that hearing sounds of a healthy oyster reef caused some groups of oyster larvae to be \"distressed,\" or to experience difficulty or begin to decline. Instead, the text indicates that the sounds increased the likelihood that groups of larvae would establish a reef (a permanent home), suggesting that the sounds had a positive effect on the larvae.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323032,"updateDate":1730147323032}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323032, 1730147323032),
    ($SATQ$0462dac3$SATQ$, $SATQ$a4391066-927e-4668-af32-7c66692cdac0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Barring major archaeological discoveries, we are unlikely to ever have <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> account of ancient Egypt under the female pharaoh Hatshepsut, as much of the evidence of her reign was deliberately destroyed by her successors.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"25a12bf8-2fde-4702-8b8d-e8b1ab409e7b","content":"<p>an imaginative</p>"},{"id":"b85b76aa-4865-42ec-bd16-5e9203231e0c","content":"<p>a superficial</p>"},{"id":"669dfa0e-75f1-4c11-9463-585138b93965","content":"<p>an exhaustive</p>"},{"id":"46aa1087-2bfa-44b3-978a-8b3e017795d4","content":"<p>a questionable</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["669dfa0e-75f1-4c11-9463-585138b93965"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of historical evidence about ancient Egypt under the reign of the pharaoh Hatshepsut. In this context, "an exhaustive" account would be a thorough one. The text states that much of the evidence from her reign was purposely destroyed&mdash;in other words, there is a lack of surviving records. This context conveys that unless there are major new archaeological discoveries, an exhaustive account of Hatshepsut&rsquo;s reign is unlikely.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, "an imaginative" account would be an account based on imagination, or ideas and speculation, rather than facts. The text indicates that much of the evidence of Hatshepsut&rsquo;s reign was deliberately destroyed, and a lack of evidence actually makes it more likely that accounts will be imaginative to some degree and not strictly factual. Choice B is incorrect because in this context, "a superficial" account would be one that is lacking in depth or concerned only with what is obvious. The text indicates that most evidence of Hatshepsut&rsquo;s reign was purposely destroyed, which suggests that accounts of that time are likely already somewhat superficial, since there is little information available to support deeper knowledge. Further, it would be illogical to suggest that discovering major new evidence would make it more likely that accounts would be superficial. Choice D is incorrect because "a questionable" account would be one likely to be challenged or doubted, and since the text suggests that little evidence of Hatshepsut&rsquo;s reign has survived, accounts of that time probably involve some speculation and thus may already be open to doubt. Further, it would be illogical to suggest that discovering major new evidence would make it more likely that accounts would be questionable.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0462dac3","external_id":"a4391066-927e-4668-af32-7c66692cdac0","vaultid":null,"uId":"173889a9-d7b3-42f2-8fb1-4d83edf5a51f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Barring major archaeological discoveries, we are unlikely to ever have <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> account of ancient Egypt under the female pharaoh Hatshepsut, as much of the evidence of her reign was deliberately destroyed by her successors.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"25a12bf8-2fde-4702-8b8d-e8b1ab409e7b","content":"<p>an imaginative</p>"},{"id":"b85b76aa-4865-42ec-bd16-5e9203231e0c","content":"<p>a superficial</p>"},{"id":"669dfa0e-75f1-4c11-9463-585138b93965","content":"<p>an exhaustive</p>"},{"id":"46aa1087-2bfa-44b3-978a-8b3e017795d4","content":"<p>a questionable</p>"}],"keys":["669dfa0e-75f1-4c11-9463-585138b93965"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of historical evidence about ancient Egypt under the reign of the pharaoh Hatshepsut. In this context, \"an exhaustive\" account would be a thorough one. The text states that much of the evidence from her reign was purposely destroyed&mdash;in other words, there is a lack of surviving records. This context conveys that unless there are major new archaeological discoveries, an exhaustive account of Hatshepsut&rsquo;s reign is unlikely.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, \"an imaginative\" account would be an account based on imagination, or ideas and speculation, rather than facts. The text indicates that much of the evidence of Hatshepsut&rsquo;s reign was deliberately destroyed, and a lack of evidence actually makes it more likely that accounts will be imaginative to some degree and not strictly factual. Choice B is incorrect because in this context, \"a superficial\" account would be one that is lacking in depth or concerned only with what is obvious. The text indicates that most evidence of Hatshepsut&rsquo;s reign was purposely destroyed, which suggests that accounts of that time are likely already somewhat superficial, since there is little information available to support deeper knowledge. Further, it would be illogical to suggest that discovering major new evidence would make it more likely that accounts would be superficial. Choice D is incorrect because \"a questionable\" account would be one likely to be challenged or doubted, and since the text suggests that little evidence of Hatshepsut&rsquo;s reign has survived, accounts of that time probably involve some speculation and thus may already be open to doubt. Further, it would be illogical to suggest that discovering major new evidence would make it more likely that accounts would be questionable.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669212,"updateDate":1743430669212}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669212, 1743430669212),
    ($SATQ$04e4c51d$SATQ$, $SATQ$7e0b7d3a-ada6-4213-90bd-2c0eed52f2a6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Taking photographs in the mid-1800s was complicated and expensive, but this changed with the <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1854</span> invention of the <span lang="fr"><em>carte de visite</em></span>, a small photo that cost little to make. <span lang="fr"><em>Carte de visite</em></span> photos helped to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> photography: they made it easy and enjoyable for everyday people to have their pictures taken, and people at the time loved exchanging these small<em> </em>photos with friends and family.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"40ebcadd-bce0-471e-9b99-e9372599c5bc","content":"<p>weaken</p>"},{"id":"5f1bcf44-6dde-41da-a3dd-61864d276f2a","content":"<p>praise</p>"},{"id":"ff065113-523f-48e1-ac22-9c58903a9819","content":"<p>popularize</p>"},{"id":"14b44fe9-b848-4bdb-b0ae-0d5785fc023e","content":"<p>isolate</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ff065113-523f-48e1-ac22-9c58903a9819"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of <em><span lang="fr">carte de visite</span></em> photos. To "popularize" a technology is to allow it to be used and appreciated widely. The text explains that unlike the photos produced by earlier forms of photographic technology, <em><span lang="fr">carte de visite</span></em> photos were inexpensive and could easily be obtained by "everyday people," who enjoyed exchanging the images. Therefore, <em><span lang="fr">carte de visite</span></em> photos helped popularize photography.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text indicates that instead of weakening the emerging technology of photography, <em><span lang="fr">carte de visite</span></em> photos allowed it to be more widely accessed and enjoyed by people. Choice B is incorrect. The text establishes that large numbers of people enjoyed using <em><span lang="fr">carte de visite</span></em> photos, so it can be inferred that these photos caused photography to be praised, or celebrated. However, it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that inanimate objects&mdash;in this case, photos&mdash;had praised photography; instead, <em><span lang="fr">carte de visite</span></em> consumers themselves would have praised it. Choice D is incorrect because the text explains that rather than isolating photography, or limiting its availability, <em><span lang="fr">carte de visite</span></em> technology made photography more widely accessible to people.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"04e4c51d","external_id":"7e0b7d3a-ada6-4213-90bd-2c0eed52f2a6","vaultid":null,"uId":"79048ffb-7459-47b0-bdc9-b0935f1e25dd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Taking photographs in the mid-1800s was complicated and expensive, but this changed with the <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1854</span> invention of the <span lang=\"fr\"><em>carte de visite</em></span>, a small photo that cost little to make. <span lang=\"fr\"><em>Carte de visite</em></span> photos helped to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> photography: they made it easy and enjoyable for everyday people to have their pictures taken, and people at the time loved exchanging these small<em> </em>photos with friends and family.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"40ebcadd-bce0-471e-9b99-e9372599c5bc","content":"<p>weaken</p>"},{"id":"5f1bcf44-6dde-41da-a3dd-61864d276f2a","content":"<p>praise</p>"},{"id":"ff065113-523f-48e1-ac22-9c58903a9819","content":"<p>popularize</p>"},{"id":"14b44fe9-b848-4bdb-b0ae-0d5785fc023e","content":"<p>isolate</p>"}],"keys":["ff065113-523f-48e1-ac22-9c58903a9819"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of <em><span lang=\"fr\">carte de visite</span></em> photos. To \"popularize\" a technology is to allow it to be used and appreciated widely. The text explains that unlike the photos produced by earlier forms of photographic technology, <em><span lang=\"fr\">carte de visite</span></em> photos were inexpensive and could easily be obtained by \"everyday people,\" who enjoyed exchanging the images. Therefore, <em><span lang=\"fr\">carte de visite</span></em> photos helped popularize photography.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text indicates that instead of weakening the emerging technology of photography, <em><span lang=\"fr\">carte de visite</span></em> photos allowed it to be more widely accessed and enjoyed by people. Choice B is incorrect. The text establishes that large numbers of people enjoyed using <em><span lang=\"fr\">carte de visite</span></em> photos, so it can be inferred that these photos caused photography to be praised, or celebrated. However, it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that inanimate objects&mdash;in this case, photos&mdash;had praised photography; instead, <em><span lang=\"fr\">carte de visite</span></em> consumers themselves would have praised it. Choice D is incorrect because the text explains that rather than isolating photography, or limiting its availability, <em><span lang=\"fr\">carte de visite</span></em> technology made photography more widely accessible to people.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669210,"updateDate":1743430669210}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669210, 1743430669210),
    ($SATQ$051d3065$SATQ$, $SATQ$3cd24546-49c6-488b-9d02-69dded3a7cd8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Kelp forests grow underwater along the eastern Pacific Coast. These underwater forests are important to fish and other marine animals. Ocean currents can be powerful and rough, making it difficult for animals to find safe places to hide from predators. The underwater forests slow down the currents. This creates a more <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> environment with calmer waters where animals can take shelter.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"34daf10f-0938-4fe9-89e2-d3ede74783de","content":"<p>tranquil</p>"},{"id":"2977e795-0313-4da6-b7e3-80ff4fbc79db","content":"<p>dangerous</p>"},{"id":"f679ceed-7968-4a45-9a0a-864c1cb742b8","content":"<p>imaginative</p>"},{"id":"ac451bd3-cfc4-4c14-b919-51e1da3b7ffb","content":"<p>surprising</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["34daf10f-0938-4fe9-89e2-d3ede74783de"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how kelp forests help marine animals. In this context, "tranquil" means free from disturbance, or calm. The text indicates that ocean currents are powerful and make it difficult for marine animals to hide from predators and that kelp forests slow currents down to create calmer areas. In other words, kelp forests create a more tranquil environment.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that kelp forests provide shelter for marine animals, meaning they create environments that are safer, not more "dangerous." Choice C is incorrect because the text discusses how kelp forests affect currents in the ocean, and it isn&rsquo;t clear how an ocean environment could be "imaginative," or full of imagination. Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that kelp forests create a calm and safe environment, and an environment that is "surprising" would be characterized by unexpected occurrences, not calmness.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"051d3065","external_id":"3cd24546-49c6-488b-9d02-69dded3a7cd8","vaultid":null,"uId":"d9e5cde5-0863-4482-b3af-e7f656bcbcf6","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Kelp forests grow underwater along the eastern Pacific Coast. These underwater forests are important to fish and other marine animals. Ocean currents can be powerful and rough, making it difficult for animals to find safe places to hide from predators. The underwater forests slow down the currents. This creates a more <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> environment with calmer waters where animals can take shelter.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"34daf10f-0938-4fe9-89e2-d3ede74783de","content":"<p>tranquil</p>"},{"id":"2977e795-0313-4da6-b7e3-80ff4fbc79db","content":"<p>dangerous</p>"},{"id":"f679ceed-7968-4a45-9a0a-864c1cb742b8","content":"<p>imaginative</p>"},{"id":"ac451bd3-cfc4-4c14-b919-51e1da3b7ffb","content":"<p>surprising</p>"}],"keys":["34daf10f-0938-4fe9-89e2-d3ede74783de"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how kelp forests help marine animals. In this context, \"tranquil\" means free from disturbance, or calm. The text indicates that ocean currents are powerful and make it difficult for marine animals to hide from predators and that kelp forests slow currents down to create calmer areas. In other words, kelp forests create a more tranquil environment.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that kelp forests provide shelter for marine animals, meaning they create environments that are safer, not more \"dangerous.\" Choice C is incorrect because the text discusses how kelp forests affect currents in the ocean, and it isn&rsquo;t clear how an ocean environment could be \"imaginative,\" or full of imagination. Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that kelp forests create a calm and safe environment, and an environment that is \"surprising\" would be characterized by unexpected occurrences, not calmness.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669205,"updateDate":1743430669205}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669205, 1743430669205),
    ($SATQ$05575bca$SATQ$, $SATQ$2b0ec1b9-82bf-48d1-9817-b1fc4aaab9b6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Lewis Carroll&rsquo;s 1871 novel <em>Through the Looking-Glass, and What Alice Found There</em>. Alice, a child, is talking to her cat.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&ldquo;Do you hear the snow against the window-panes, Kitty? How nice and <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>soft</u></span> it sounds! Just as if someone was kissing the window all over outside.&rdquo;</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;soft&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b2801f09-b8f9-4e48-ba3d-9567fe4cda26","content":"<p>Gentle</p>"},{"id":"ac4a5ecc-7fee-474a-ac87-7435347c1913","content":"<p>Sensitive</p>"},{"id":"c3717c6a-daa8-4df4-a2c3-ef3e0f032ad3","content":"<p>Shapeless</p>"},{"id":"92990af6-774e-4cd3-9ed8-573eb4ab1ccd","content":"<p>Bland</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b2801f09-b8f9-4e48-ba3d-9567fe4cda26"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, "soft" most nearly means gentle. The text portrays Alice speaking about the snow falling against the window, describing it as sounding "nice" and similar to someone "kissing the window." These details most strongly suggest that Alice thinks the sound made by the snow falling against the window is gentle.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts, "gentle" can mean sensitive, or caring about others&rsquo; feelings, that isn&rsquo;t the meaning in this context; it doesn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Alice thinks the sound of the snow cares about anything. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Alice thinks the sound of the snow is shapeless, or lacks a definite shape; she isn&rsquo;t indicating how anything looks or feels but, rather, is describing how "nice" and gentle the snow sounds. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Alice thinks the sound of the snow is bland, or uninteresting; rather, she notices the sound and comments on how "nice" it is.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"05575bca","external_id":"2b0ec1b9-82bf-48d1-9817-b1fc4aaab9b6","vaultid":null,"uId":"742108e5-638d-4491-84b5-cd9e54bcfd62","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Lewis Carroll&rsquo;s 1871 novel <em>Through the Looking-Glass, and What Alice Found There</em>. Alice, a child, is talking to her cat.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&ldquo;Do you hear the snow against the window-panes, Kitty? How nice and <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>soft</u></span> it sounds! Just as if someone was kissing the window all over outside.&rdquo;</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;soft&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b2801f09-b8f9-4e48-ba3d-9567fe4cda26","content":"<p>Gentle</p>"},{"id":"ac4a5ecc-7fee-474a-ac87-7435347c1913","content":"<p>Sensitive</p>"},{"id":"c3717c6a-daa8-4df4-a2c3-ef3e0f032ad3","content":"<p>Shapeless</p>"},{"id":"92990af6-774e-4cd3-9ed8-573eb4ab1ccd","content":"<p>Bland</p>"}],"keys":["b2801f09-b8f9-4e48-ba3d-9567fe4cda26"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, \"soft\" most nearly means gentle. The text portrays Alice speaking about the snow falling against the window, describing it as sounding \"nice\" and similar to someone \"kissing the window.\" These details most strongly suggest that Alice thinks the sound made by the snow falling against the window is gentle.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts, \"gentle\" can mean sensitive, or caring about others&rsquo; feelings, that isn&rsquo;t the meaning in this context; it doesn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Alice thinks the sound of the snow cares about anything. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Alice thinks the sound of the snow is shapeless, or lacks a definite shape; she isn&rsquo;t indicating how anything looks or feels but, rather, is describing how \"nice\" and gentle the snow sounds. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Alice thinks the sound of the snow is bland, or uninteresting; rather, she notices the sound and comments on how \"nice\" it is.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323031,"updateDate":1730147323031}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323031, 1730147323031),
    ($SATQ$059f7201$SATQ$, $SATQ$90a4a88d-8b15-48a0-9609-1f6769ed7df0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level="2" role="heading"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Graphic novels are increasingly popular in bookstores and libraries, but they shouldn&rsquo;t be classified as literature. By definition, literature tells a story or conveys meaning through language only; graphic novels tell stories through illustrations and use language only sparingly, in captions and dialogue. Graphic novels are experienced as series of images and not as language, making them more similar to film than to literature.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level="2" role="heading"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>Graphic novels present their stories through both language and images. Without captions and dialogue, readers would be unable to understand what is depicted in the illustrations: the story results from the interaction of text and image. Moreover, Alison Bechdel&rsquo;s <em>Fun Home</em> and many other graphic novels feature text that is as beautifully written as the prose found in many standard novels. Therefore, graphic novels qualify as literary texts.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the overall argument presented in Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"90b8e8b9-aa03-463b-82be-9b71ebb2d5cd","content":"<p>By asserting that language plays a more important role in graphic novels than the author of Text 1 recognizes</p>"},{"id":"1a2bd4fc-c8fd-415c-b67f-74df939dc216","content":"<p>By acknowledging that the author of Text 1 has identified a flaw that is common to all graphic novels</p>"},{"id":"44beb725-e879-4269-9767-710a0ab684a8","content":"<p>By suggesting that the story lines of certain graphic novels are more difficult to understand than the author of Text 1 claims</p>"},{"id":"1b07e188-d3f4-46fb-8442-27623c540ff1","content":"<p>By agreeing with the author of Text 1 that most graphic novels aren&rsquo;t as well crafted as most literary works are</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["90b8e8b9-aa03-463b-82be-9b71ebb2d5cd"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately states how the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to the argument presented in Text 1. In arguing that graphic novels shouldn&rsquo;t be classified as literature and are more comparable to film, Text 1 claims that language plays a relatively limited role in graphic novels: images, not language, are the primary means by which graphic novels tell their stories, and language is used "only sparingly"&mdash;that is, comparatively very little&mdash;in the form of captions and dialogue. However, the author of Text 2 asserts that language in graphic novels is as equally vital for conveying meaning as images are, since without captions and dialogue, readers wouldn&rsquo;t be able to make sense of the narrative. Moreover, the author of Text 2 argues that there are many graphic novels that are "beautifully written" and whose use of language is as accomplished as any standard novel. Because Text 1 argues that language is subordinate to images in graphic novels, whereas Text 2 highlights how language is an essential component of a graphic novel&rsquo;s storytelling, it can reasonably be inferred that the author of Text 2 would say that language plays a more important role in graphic novels than the author of Text 1 recognizes.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although Text 1 indicates that graphic novels shouldn&rsquo;t be classified as literature based on their limited use of language, there&rsquo;s no indication that the author of Text 1 considers this limited use of language as a flaw, just that it doesn&rsquo;t fit the particular definition of "literature" proposed in the text. Even if Text 1 had suggested that their use of language was a common flaw of graphic novels, the author of Text 2 emphasizes how many graphic novels are "beautifully written," and would therefore say that their use of language is exemplary, not that it is flawed. Choice C is incorrect because Text 1 doesn&rsquo;t claim that the story lines of graphic novels are generally relatively easy to understand; in addition, Text 2 argues that given their dependence on the interaction of image and text, the stories of graphic novels would be incomprehensible if their captions and dialogue were removed, not that the story lines of some graphic novels are more difficult to understand than Text 1 acknowledges. Choice D is incorrect because the author of Text 1 doesn&rsquo;t imply that graphic novels aren&rsquo;t well crafted, only that they use language too sparingly to fit the definition of "literature," and that their use of images to convey stories makes them more comparable to film than to literature. Even if the author of Text 1 had implied that most graphic novels aren&rsquo;t well crafted, Text 2 refers to the fact that many graphic novels are as beautifully written&mdash;that is, well crafted&mdash;as many standard novels; thus, it wouldn&rsquo;t be accurate to say that the author of Text 2 would agree with the author of Text 1 that most graphic novels aren&rsquo;t well crafted.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"059f7201","external_id":"90a4a88d-8b15-48a0-9609-1f6769ed7df0","vaultid":null,"uId":"3f73e91b-b021-43cd-a79d-fe48da2560ff","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level=\"2\" role=\"heading\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Graphic novels are increasingly popular in bookstores and libraries, but they shouldn&rsquo;t be classified as literature. By definition, literature tells a story or conveys meaning through language only; graphic novels tell stories through illustrations and use language only sparingly, in captions and dialogue. Graphic novels are experienced as series of images and not as language, making them more similar to film than to literature.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level=\"2\" role=\"heading\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>Graphic novels present their stories through both language and images. Without captions and dialogue, readers would be unable to understand what is depicted in the illustrations: the story results from the interaction of text and image. Moreover, Alison Bechdel&rsquo;s <em>Fun Home</em> and many other graphic novels feature text that is as beautifully written as the prose found in many standard novels. Therefore, graphic novels qualify as literary texts.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the overall argument presented in Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"90b8e8b9-aa03-463b-82be-9b71ebb2d5cd","content":"<p>By asserting that language plays a more important role in graphic novels than the author of Text 1 recognizes</p>"},{"id":"1a2bd4fc-c8fd-415c-b67f-74df939dc216","content":"<p>By acknowledging that the author of Text 1 has identified a flaw that is common to all graphic novels</p>"},{"id":"44beb725-e879-4269-9767-710a0ab684a8","content":"<p>By suggesting that the story lines of certain graphic novels are more difficult to understand than the author of Text 1 claims</p>"},{"id":"1b07e188-d3f4-46fb-8442-27623c540ff1","content":"<p>By agreeing with the author of Text 1 that most graphic novels aren&rsquo;t as well crafted as most literary works are</p>"}],"keys":["90b8e8b9-aa03-463b-82be-9b71ebb2d5cd"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately states how the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to the argument presented in Text 1. In arguing that graphic novels shouldn&rsquo;t be classified as literature and are more comparable to film, Text 1 claims that language plays a relatively limited role in graphic novels: images, not language, are the primary means by which graphic novels tell their stories, and language is used \"only sparingly\"&mdash;that is, comparatively very little&mdash;in the form of captions and dialogue. However, the author of Text 2 asserts that language in graphic novels is as equally vital for conveying meaning as images are, since without captions and dialogue, readers wouldn&rsquo;t be able to make sense of the narrative. Moreover, the author of Text 2 argues that there are many graphic novels that are \"beautifully written\" and whose use of language is as accomplished as any standard novel. Because Text 1 argues that language is subordinate to images in graphic novels, whereas Text 2 highlights how language is an essential component of a graphic novel&rsquo;s storytelling, it can reasonably be inferred that the author of Text 2 would say that language plays a more important role in graphic novels than the author of Text 1 recognizes.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although Text 1 indicates that graphic novels shouldn&rsquo;t be classified as literature based on their limited use of language, there&rsquo;s no indication that the author of Text 1 considers this limited use of language as a flaw, just that it doesn&rsquo;t fit the particular definition of \"literature\" proposed in the text. Even if Text 1 had suggested that their use of language was a common flaw of graphic novels, the author of Text 2 emphasizes how many graphic novels are \"beautifully written,\" and would therefore say that their use of language is exemplary, not that it is flawed. Choice C is incorrect because Text 1 doesn&rsquo;t claim that the story lines of graphic novels are generally relatively easy to understand; in addition, Text 2 argues that given their dependence on the interaction of image and text, the stories of graphic novels would be incomprehensible if their captions and dialogue were removed, not that the story lines of some graphic novels are more difficult to understand than Text 1 acknowledges. Choice D is incorrect because the author of Text 1 doesn&rsquo;t imply that graphic novels aren&rsquo;t well crafted, only that they use language too sparingly to fit the definition of \"literature,\" and that their use of images to convey stories makes them more comparable to film than to literature. Even if the author of Text 1 had implied that most graphic novels aren&rsquo;t well crafted, Text 2 refers to the fact that many graphic novels are as beautifully written&mdash;that is, well crafted&mdash;as many standard novels; thus, it wouldn&rsquo;t be accurate to say that the author of Text 2 would agree with the author of Text 1 that most graphic novels aren&rsquo;t well crafted.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323032,"updateDate":1730147323032}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323032, 1730147323032),
    ($SATQ$066a3295$SATQ$, $SATQ$719a1f6e-60f0-4416-866e-481a6f564467$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$430c549b-2138-460d-b076-60d8b09565b6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Researchers have found a nearly 164,000-year-old molar from a member of the archaic human species known as Denisovans in a cave in Laos, suggesting that Denisovans lived in a wider range of environments than indicated by earlier evidence. <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>Before the discovery, Denisovans were thought to have lived only at high altitudes in relatively cold climates in what are now Russia and China</u></span>, but the discovery of the tooth in Laos suggests that they may have lived at low altitudes in relatively warm climates in Southeast Asia as well.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a37af8f6-081e-45af-b7a5-c7c3376b21bd","content":"<p>It dismisses as untrue the research presented in the previous sentence.</p>"},{"id":"ad1df3ad-c4d4-432a-90d5-71f213b4d6e1","content":"<p>It defines a term used in the description that follows in the rest of the sentence.</p>"},{"id":"3f9f3f7f-7294-4c9a-99e8-a82150f4a19d","content":"<p>It emphasizes the main goal of the research introduced in the previous sentence.</p>"},{"id":"ae36107b-bd26-42d7-b0da-49163aabf792","content":"<p>It provides context that clarifies the significance of the information that follows in the rest of the sentence.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ae36107b-bd26-42d7-b0da-49163aabf792"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The text describes how a new discovery expands our understanding of Denisovans. The underlined portion describes what we used to believe about Denisovans, which helps the reader understand the significance of the discovery of the molar: it suggests that they lived in more places than we&rsquo;d previously thought. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t do this. Instead, it explains what we used to believe about Denisovans before the discovery&mdash;it doesn&rsquo;t dismiss the new discovery as false. Choice B is incorrect. The underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t do this. No term is defined here. Choice C is incorrect. The underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t do this. The text never tells us what the &ldquo;goal&rdquo; of the research was, just what its discovery was. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-E003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b625548e-6b2d-4591-8522-2526b4b573c1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"066a3295","external_id":"719a1f6e-60f0-4416-866e-481a6f564467","vaultid":"430c549b-2138-460d-b076-60d8b09565b6","uId":"1c7c3a67-8305-40af-b1a1-8c1a30c3ab08","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Researchers have found a nearly 164,000-year-old molar from a member of the archaic human species known as Denisovans in a cave in Laos, suggesting that Denisovans lived in a wider range of environments than indicated by earlier evidence. <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>Before the discovery, Denisovans were thought to have lived only at high altitudes in relatively cold climates in what are now Russia and China</u></span>, but the discovery of the tooth in Laos suggests that they may have lived at low altitudes in relatively warm climates in Southeast Asia as well.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a37af8f6-081e-45af-b7a5-c7c3376b21bd","content":"<p>It dismisses as untrue the research presented in the previous sentence.</p>"},{"id":"ad1df3ad-c4d4-432a-90d5-71f213b4d6e1","content":"<p>It defines a term used in the description that follows in the rest of the sentence.</p>"},{"id":"3f9f3f7f-7294-4c9a-99e8-a82150f4a19d","content":"<p>It emphasizes the main goal of the research introduced in the previous sentence.</p>"},{"id":"ae36107b-bd26-42d7-b0da-49163aabf792","content":"<p>It provides context that clarifies the significance of the information that follows in the rest of the sentence.</p>"}],"keys":["ae36107b-bd26-42d7-b0da-49163aabf792"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The text describes how a new discovery expands our understanding of Denisovans. The underlined portion describes what we used to believe about Denisovans, which helps the reader understand the significance of the discovery of the molar: it suggests that they lived in more places than we&rsquo;d previously thought. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t do this. Instead, it explains what we used to believe about Denisovans before the discovery&mdash;it doesn&rsquo;t dismiss the new discovery as false. Choice B is incorrect. The underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t do this. No term is defined here. Choice C is incorrect. The underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t do this. The text never tells us what the &ldquo;goal&rdquo; of the research was, just what its discovery was. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-E003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b625548e-6b2d-4591-8522-2526b4b573c1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959374,"updateDate":1691007959374}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959374, 1691007959374),
    ($SATQ$06b96bfc$SATQ$, $SATQ$608d2837-f8c9-4257-876b-4c2faa74fb4b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b4e9204d-7698-489a-a27a-f9292faa9b6c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A musician and member of the Quechua of Peru, Renata Flores Rivera was eager to promote the Quechua language in her music, but she was <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> speaking it. She met this challenge by asking her grandmother, a native speaker of Quechua, to help her pronounce words in her song lyrics and also by taking classes in the language.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"68153d44-570a-46a9-b84d-279ab03d705d","content":"<p>prepared for</p>"},{"id":"aec9d97c-cb0d-4348-afe8-0381fe6a0dd1","content":"<p>inexperienced with</p>"},{"id":"f695e14b-947e-4aaa-967f-86b96477e3ad","content":"<p>skilled in</p>"},{"id":"f4331e0e-01c7-4b57-a326-551a4778aa25","content":"<p>excited about</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["aec9d97c-cb0d-4348-afe8-0381fe6a0dd1"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Renata Flores Rivera&rsquo;s use of Quechua in her music. In this context, &ldquo;inexperienced with&rdquo; means not accustomed to. The text indicates that Flores Rivera wanted to promote the Quechua language in her music and overcame a challenge by seeking help with pronunciation from her grandmother and by taking language classes. This context conveys the idea that Flores Rivera was not sufficiently familiar with Quechua to use it in her music without help. Thus, she was inexperienced with speaking the language, which she addressed by seeking help. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because describing Flores Rivera as &ldquo;prepared for&rdquo;&mdash;or ready for&mdash;speaking Quechua wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text indicates that speaking Quechua presented a challenge, but if she were ready to speak the language, then there would be no challenge. Choice C is incorrect because describing Flores Rivera as &ldquo;skilled in&rdquo;&mdash;meaning good at or capable of&mdash;speaking Quechua wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text indicates that speaking Quechua presented a challenge, but if she were capable of speaking the language, then there would be no challenge. Choice D is incorrect. Flores Rivera was likely &ldquo;excited about&rdquo;&mdash;or thrilled or delighted with&mdash;speaking Quechua, but this wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text indicates that speaking Quechua presented a challenge, but if she were delighted with speaking the language, then there would be no challenge. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-043-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1a7cc4f2-08dc-4df0-adc1-7fa3a3cf1c82$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"06b96bfc","external_id":"608d2837-f8c9-4257-876b-4c2faa74fb4b","vaultid":"b4e9204d-7698-489a-a27a-f9292faa9b6c","uId":"0e47f76e-0d25-4ca4-ae19-0b0b2185de8d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A musician and member of the Quechua of Peru, Renata Flores Rivera was eager to promote the Quechua language in her music, but she was <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> speaking it. She met this challenge by asking her grandmother, a native speaker of Quechua, to help her pronounce words in her song lyrics and also by taking classes in the language.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"68153d44-570a-46a9-b84d-279ab03d705d","content":"<p>prepared for</p>"},{"id":"aec9d97c-cb0d-4348-afe8-0381fe6a0dd1","content":"<p>inexperienced with</p>"},{"id":"f695e14b-947e-4aaa-967f-86b96477e3ad","content":"<p>skilled in</p>"},{"id":"f4331e0e-01c7-4b57-a326-551a4778aa25","content":"<p>excited about</p>"}],"keys":["aec9d97c-cb0d-4348-afe8-0381fe6a0dd1"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Renata Flores Rivera&rsquo;s use of Quechua in her music. In this context, &ldquo;inexperienced with&rdquo; means not accustomed to. The text indicates that Flores Rivera wanted to promote the Quechua language in her music and overcame a challenge by seeking help with pronunciation from her grandmother and by taking language classes. This context conveys the idea that Flores Rivera was not sufficiently familiar with Quechua to use it in her music without help. Thus, she was inexperienced with speaking the language, which she addressed by seeking help. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because describing Flores Rivera as &ldquo;prepared for&rdquo;&mdash;or ready for&mdash;speaking Quechua wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text indicates that speaking Quechua presented a challenge, but if she were ready to speak the language, then there would be no challenge. Choice C is incorrect because describing Flores Rivera as &ldquo;skilled in&rdquo;&mdash;meaning good at or capable of&mdash;speaking Quechua wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text indicates that speaking Quechua presented a challenge, but if she were capable of speaking the language, then there would be no challenge. Choice D is incorrect. Flores Rivera was likely &ldquo;excited about&rdquo;&mdash;or thrilled or delighted with&mdash;speaking Quechua, but this wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text indicates that speaking Quechua presented a challenge, but if she were delighted with speaking the language, then there would be no challenge. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-043-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1a7cc4f2-08dc-4df0-adc1-7fa3a3cf1c82","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959322,"updateDate":1691007959322}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959322, 1691007959322),
    ($SATQ$0a04cac5$SATQ$, $SATQ$5a6b4fb8-c7e5-4ce9-84ba-1d44b4be1dda$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e7101ddd-b6ba-4981-a282-28432cfb348f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Jane Austen&rsquo;s 1814 novel <em>Mansfield Park</em>. The speaker, Tom, is considering staging a play at home with a group of his friends and family.</p>
<p style="padding-left:1em;">We mean nothing but a little amusement among ourselves, just to vary the scene, and exercise our powers in something new. We want no audience, no publicity. We may be trusted, I think, in choosing some play most perfectly unexceptionable; and I can conceive no greater harm or danger to any of us in conversing in the elegant written language of some respectable author than in chattering in words of our own.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text? </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a2a014b0-d081-46d6-a856-cadaa40b356d","content":"<p>To offer Tom&rsquo;s assurance that the play will be inoffensive and involve only a small number of people</p>"},{"id":"aafc5629-3b8e-428c-854b-642d99f81170","content":"<p>To clarify that the play will not be performed in the manner Tom had originally intended</p>"},{"id":"04bf1884-67f1-475a-81b5-0009d1d6a861","content":"<p>To elaborate on the idea that the people around Tom lack the skills to successfully stage a play</p>"},{"id":"e30eea8d-c1af-453c-9c5e-2b5ccee8a8f0","content":"<p>To assert that Tom believes the group performing the play will be able to successfully promote it</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a2a014b0-d081-46d6-a856-cadaa40b356d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately portrays the main purpose of the text. At the beginning of the text, Tom asserts that he and the other people staging the play are doing so only for &ldquo;a little amusement among ourselves&rdquo; and aren&rsquo;t interested in attracting an audience or any attention with the production. Then, Tom promises that the play they chose is modest and appropriate, and he further reasons that using the well-written prose of &ldquo;some respectable author&rdquo; is better than using their own words. Overall, the main purpose of the text is to convey Tom&rsquo;s promise that the play will be inoffensive and involve only a few people. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Tom had earlier intentions for the play&rsquo;s performance or that anything has changed since the group first decided to stage a play. Instead, the text focuses on how harmless the entire endeavor will be.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. Although Tom mentions that using the words of a &ldquo;respectable author&rdquo; will be better than using their own words, he never addresses the idea that the people around him generally aren&rsquo;t skilled enough to stage a play. Choice D is incorrect because in the text Tom specifically says that they &ldquo;want no audience, no publicity,&rdquo; which indicates that they don&rsquo;t plan on promoting the play at all.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-033-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b83d57e5-bbce-46f8-8de3-f0c0f83e28c1$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0a04cac5","external_id":"5a6b4fb8-c7e5-4ce9-84ba-1d44b4be1dda","vaultid":"e7101ddd-b6ba-4981-a282-28432cfb348f","uId":"aff6e638-9bf2-42d9-b22c-e7d9483b72ce","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Jane Austen&rsquo;s 1814 novel <em>Mansfield Park</em>. The speaker, Tom, is considering staging a play at home with a group of his friends and family.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left:1em;\">We mean nothing but a little amusement among ourselves, just to vary the scene, and exercise our powers in something new. We want no audience, no publicity. We may be trusted, I think, in choosing some play most perfectly unexceptionable; and I can conceive no greater harm or danger to any of us in conversing in the elegant written language of some respectable author than in chattering in words of our own.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text? </p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a2a014b0-d081-46d6-a856-cadaa40b356d","content":"<p>To offer Tom&rsquo;s assurance that the play will be inoffensive and involve only a small number of people</p>"},{"id":"aafc5629-3b8e-428c-854b-642d99f81170","content":"<p>To clarify that the play will not be performed in the manner Tom had originally intended</p>"},{"id":"04bf1884-67f1-475a-81b5-0009d1d6a861","content":"<p>To elaborate on the idea that the people around Tom lack the skills to successfully stage a play</p>"},{"id":"e30eea8d-c1af-453c-9c5e-2b5ccee8a8f0","content":"<p>To assert that Tom believes the group performing the play will be able to successfully promote it</p>"}],"keys":["a2a014b0-d081-46d6-a856-cadaa40b356d"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately portrays the main purpose of the text. At the beginning of the text, Tom asserts that he and the other people staging the play are doing so only for &ldquo;a little amusement among ourselves&rdquo; and aren&rsquo;t interested in attracting an audience or any attention with the production. Then, Tom promises that the play they chose is modest and appropriate, and he further reasons that using the well-written prose of &ldquo;some respectable author&rdquo; is better than using their own words. Overall, the main purpose of the text is to convey Tom&rsquo;s promise that the play will be inoffensive and involve only a few people. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Tom had earlier intentions for the play&rsquo;s performance or that anything has changed since the group first decided to stage a play. Instead, the text focuses on how harmless the entire endeavor will be.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. Although Tom mentions that using the words of a &ldquo;respectable author&rdquo; will be better than using their own words, he never addresses the idea that the people around him generally aren&rsquo;t skilled enough to stage a play. Choice D is incorrect because in the text Tom specifically says that they &ldquo;want no audience, no publicity,&rdquo; which indicates that they don&rsquo;t plan on promoting the play at all.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-033-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b83d57e5-bbce-46f8-8de3-f0c0f83e28c1","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959319,"updateDate":1691007959319}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959319, 1691007959319),
    ($SATQ$0afb2ac4$SATQ$, $SATQ$e2330828-1418-4ad0-8880-e2467492c024$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Ancient Inca inhabiting the Andes Mountains used terraces, rows of flattened land with built-in irrigation systems, to grow crops at different altitudes. This method of farming proved to be highly <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>, as evidenced by the great number and variety of crops grown at that time.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fc521fba-00e3-4882-b8cb-cc34c2ff03c6","content":"<p>chaotic</p>"},{"id":"850ae3c2-e899-46cf-9816-9506b81f1a02","content":"<p>uniform</p>"},{"id":"0b76b213-8151-494e-82e3-277619d41d2e","content":"<p>effective</p>"},{"id":"fe7a1653-eac8-4c74-9c15-15cdfb971214","content":"<p>burdensome</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0b76b213-8151-494e-82e3-277619d41d2e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the ancient Inca farming method. In this context, &ldquo;effective&rdquo; means successful in producing a desired result. The text describes how the Inca used terraces with built-in irrigation systems to grow crops at different altitudes and then indicates that this farming method produced positive results &ldquo;as evidenced by the great number and variety of crops grown at that time.&rdquo; The context clearly suggests that the terraced farming approach was highly productive, or effective.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because to describe the Inca farming method as &ldquo;chaotic&rdquo; contradicts the text&rsquo;s description of a systematic approach using deliberately constructed terraces with built-in irrigation systems. Moreover, the &ldquo;great number and variety of crops&rdquo; yielded by this method seems unlikely to result from a disorganized, or chaotic, approach. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;uniform&rdquo; means consistent or the same throughout. It does not make sense in this context to describe a farming method as highly uniform if it produced a &ldquo;great number and variety of crops.&rdquo; Moreover, the text indicates that Inca terrace farming was specifically designed to accommodate different crops at different altitudes, highlighting the diversity rather than uniformity of this approach. Choice D is incorrect because the success of the method, &ldquo;as evidenced by the great number and variety of crops grown,&rdquo; does not support the claim that the Inca farming method was &ldquo;burdensome,&rdquo; or difficult to implement or maintain. In fact, the context clearly emphasizes the advantages, rather than the burdens, of Inca terrace farming.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0afb2ac4","external_id":"e2330828-1418-4ad0-8880-e2467492c024","vaultid":null,"uId":"d81eb2bb-7189-4920-a235-4276e1601c2d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Ancient Inca inhabiting the Andes Mountains used terraces, rows of flattened land with built-in irrigation systems, to grow crops at different altitudes. This method of farming proved to be highly <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>, as evidenced by the great number and variety of crops grown at that time.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"fc521fba-00e3-4882-b8cb-cc34c2ff03c6","content":"<p>chaotic</p>"},{"id":"850ae3c2-e899-46cf-9816-9506b81f1a02","content":"<p>uniform</p>"},{"id":"0b76b213-8151-494e-82e3-277619d41d2e","content":"<p>effective</p>"},{"id":"fe7a1653-eac8-4c74-9c15-15cdfb971214","content":"<p>burdensome</p>"}],"keys":["0b76b213-8151-494e-82e3-277619d41d2e"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the ancient Inca farming method. In this context, &ldquo;effective&rdquo; means successful in producing a desired result. The text describes how the Inca used terraces with built-in irrigation systems to grow crops at different altitudes and then indicates that this farming method produced positive results &ldquo;as evidenced by the great number and variety of crops grown at that time.&rdquo; The context clearly suggests that the terraced farming approach was highly productive, or effective.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because to describe the Inca farming method as &ldquo;chaotic&rdquo; contradicts the text&rsquo;s description of a systematic approach using deliberately constructed terraces with built-in irrigation systems. Moreover, the &ldquo;great number and variety of crops&rdquo; yielded by this method seems unlikely to result from a disorganized, or chaotic, approach. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;uniform&rdquo; means consistent or the same throughout. It does not make sense in this context to describe a farming method as highly uniform if it produced a &ldquo;great number and variety of crops.&rdquo; Moreover, the text indicates that Inca terrace farming was specifically designed to accommodate different crops at different altitudes, highlighting the diversity rather than uniformity of this approach. Choice D is incorrect because the success of the method, &ldquo;as evidenced by the great number and variety of crops grown,&rdquo; does not support the claim that the Inca farming method was &ldquo;burdensome,&rdquo; or difficult to implement or maintain. In fact, the context clearly emphasizes the advantages, rather than the burdens, of Inca terrace farming.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$0b5ecf0e$SATQ$, $SATQ$8e977476-4530-4bff-ab87-72b97c08f9a2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>During Rome&rsquo;s republican period, which ended in the first century BCE, libraries were predominantly owned by wealthy individuals who tightly controlled access to their book collections. The first public library became available in Rome in 28 BCE and was soon followed by one commissioned by Emperor Augustus. As modern scholar Fabio Fernandes notes, however, these two traditions aren&rsquo;t as distinct as they seem, as both the emperor and the private library owners viewed their libraries as extensions of their personal patronage, just on vastly differing scales.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a6ee2433-10f7-4305-b33e-ac500dd8a2fa","content":"<p>To give a brief overview of public access to libraries throughout Rome&rsquo;s republican period</p>"},{"id":"3c7dbff5-f9b9-4165-887b-f6b92dd07039","content":"<p>To contend that early imperial leaders in Rome wielded too much influence over libraries</p>"},{"id":"0ba5789b-b834-4b3c-aa5d-058f35ee5309","content":"<p>To assert that private and early public libraries in ancient Rome had an essential similarity</p>"},{"id":"d69560b4-1aaf-423a-b796-281a816108d5","content":"<p>To call into question the notion that private Roman libraries disappeared during the first century BCE</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0ba5789b-b834-4b3c-aa5d-058f35ee5309"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text. The text describes two types of libraries in first-century BCE Rome&mdash;private libraries controlled by wealthy individuals, and the public libraries like that commissioned by Emperor Augustus&mdash;and then notes that "these two traditions aren&rsquo;t as distinct as they seem." It explains that both private-library owners and the emperor saw their libraries as "extensions of their personal patronage, just on vastly differing scales," because the emperor&rsquo;s patronage extended to the whole citizenry of Rome. Thus, both private and public libraries in ancient Rome were fundamentally tied to relevant principles of patronage.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text mentions that Rome&rsquo;s republican period only to say that it "ended in the first century BCE," thus it doesn&rsquo;t support any conclusions about possible occurrences "throughout Rome&rsquo;s republican period." Choice B is incorrect. The text neither discusses "early imperial leaders in Rome," nor whether their level of influence was appropriate. Rather it discusses the end of "Rome&rsquo;s republican period" in relation to libraries, who created them, and who got to use them without passing judgment on propriety. Choice D is incorrect. Although the text indicates that Rome&rsquo;s republican period "ended in the first century BCE," a period that also saw the first public library come into use (28 BCE), nothing in the text suggests that private libraries disappeared at that time.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0b5ecf0e","external_id":"8e977476-4530-4bff-ab87-72b97c08f9a2","vaultid":null,"uId":"6fb16aae-a444-4c87-81b1-7785b6a4f310","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>During Rome&rsquo;s republican period, which ended in the first century BCE, libraries were predominantly owned by wealthy individuals who tightly controlled access to their book collections. The first public library became available in Rome in 28 BCE and was soon followed by one commissioned by Emperor Augustus. As modern scholar Fabio Fernandes notes, however, these two traditions aren&rsquo;t as distinct as they seem, as both the emperor and the private library owners viewed their libraries as extensions of their personal patronage, just on vastly differing scales.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a6ee2433-10f7-4305-b33e-ac500dd8a2fa","content":"<p>To give a brief overview of public access to libraries throughout Rome&rsquo;s republican period</p>"},{"id":"3c7dbff5-f9b9-4165-887b-f6b92dd07039","content":"<p>To contend that early imperial leaders in Rome wielded too much influence over libraries</p>"},{"id":"0ba5789b-b834-4b3c-aa5d-058f35ee5309","content":"<p>To assert that private and early public libraries in ancient Rome had an essential similarity</p>"},{"id":"d69560b4-1aaf-423a-b796-281a816108d5","content":"<p>To call into question the notion that private Roman libraries disappeared during the first century BCE</p>"}],"keys":["0ba5789b-b834-4b3c-aa5d-058f35ee5309"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text. The text describes two types of libraries in first-century BCE Rome&mdash;private libraries controlled by wealthy individuals, and the public libraries like that commissioned by Emperor Augustus&mdash;and then notes that \"these two traditions aren&rsquo;t as distinct as they seem.\" It explains that both private-library owners and the emperor saw their libraries as \"extensions of their personal patronage, just on vastly differing scales,\" because the emperor&rsquo;s patronage extended to the whole citizenry of Rome. Thus, both private and public libraries in ancient Rome were fundamentally tied to relevant principles of patronage.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text mentions that Rome&rsquo;s republican period only to say that it \"ended in the first century BCE,\" thus it doesn&rsquo;t support any conclusions about possible occurrences \"throughout Rome&rsquo;s republican period.\" Choice B is incorrect. The text neither discusses \"early imperial leaders in Rome,\" nor whether their level of influence was appropriate. Rather it discusses the end of \"Rome&rsquo;s republican period\" in relation to libraries, who created them, and who got to use them without passing judgment on propriety. Choice D is incorrect. Although the text indicates that Rome&rsquo;s republican period \"ended in the first century BCE,\" a period that also saw the first public library come into use (28 BCE), nothing in the text suggests that private libraries disappeared at that time.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061189,"updateDate":1755117061189}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061189, 1755117061189),
    ($SATQ$0d402146$SATQ$, $SATQ$6f73ae1d-2a53-4dd9-b5ea-b1b1c2f3b734$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$04f194eb-e092-44dd-a130-2dbfb3ad18c9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>US traffic signals didn&rsquo;t always contain the familiar three lights (red, yellow, and green). Traffic lights only <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> red and green lights until the three-light traffic signal was developed in the 1920s.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"521871a2-c187-4385-825d-5c8f17105b2e","content":"<p>avoided</p>"},{"id":"b4c1093f-e34c-4be4-a851-dd457ba8038a","content":"<p>featured</p>"},{"id":"324816d3-9203-48f0-9a4b-2da148c29d2d","content":"<p>appreciated</p>"},{"id":"dcfb7d50-9cb7-42ed-b985-1d90f75db61e","content":"<p>disregarded</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b4c1093f-e34c-4be4-a851-dd457ba8038a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of traffic signals. As used in this context, &ldquo;featured&rdquo; means had as a characteristic or part. The text indicates that although US traffic signals have lights of three different colors (red, yellow, and green), this wasn&rsquo;t the case until the 1920s, when the three-light signal was first developed. Before then, the text suggests, traffic signals had fewer lights (as indicated by the word &ldquo;only&rdquo; in the second sentence). This context supports the idea that before the 1920s, traffic signals featured only red and green lights. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;avoided&rdquo; means kept away from someone or something or prevented something from occurring, neither of which would make sense in context. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss keeping away from someone or something but instead focuses on what US traffic signals look like. The text states that they didn&rsquo;t have lights of three different colors until the three-light signal was developed in the 1920s. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;appreciated&rdquo; means admired or increased in value, neither of which would make sense in context. The text focuses solely on the fact that US traffic signals have contained lights of three colors only since the three-light signal was developed in the 1920s. The text doesn&rsquo;t mention how this characteristic or traffic signals in general are perceived or what their value is. Moreover, the blank portion of the text describes &ldquo;traffic signals,&rdquo; which are lifeless objects and therefore unable to admire or increase the value of something.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;disregarded&rdquo; means ignored or treated something as unworthy of notice, neither of which would make sense in context. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss how people react to traffic signals; rather, it addresses the idea that US traffic signals have contained lights of three colors only since the three-light signal was developed in the 1920s. Moreover, the blank portion of the text describes &ldquo;traffic signals,&rdquo; which are lifeless objects and therefore unable to ignore or treat something in a particular manner.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-E003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e7cb3b45-4c28-4e54-b1b6-3b3a390e8647$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0d402146","external_id":"6f73ae1d-2a53-4dd9-b5ea-b1b1c2f3b734","vaultid":"04f194eb-e092-44dd-a130-2dbfb3ad18c9","uId":"a0c97ada-c8e2-474d-a784-baf1ff5dc5d2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_